1: # The exact boundary twist for a simple flop
2: 
3: Working proof, 17 November 2026. The quaternionic boundary map and the
4: explicit relative generators below are calculated here. The actual-family
5: stable derivative and the oriented identification with the run's Theta_7
6: generator still require the checks stated at the end. No arbitrary-fan
7: return value is assigned by this note.
8: 
9: ## 1. The two actual normal products
10: 
11: Write the conifold link as
12: 
13:     K=(S^3_a times S^3_c)/S^1,
14:     (a,c) ~ (e^{i phi}a,e^{-i phi}c)
15: 
16: in complex coordinates. With the quaternion convention already used in
17: the Cox calculation put
18: 
19:     u=a0+j a1,   v=c0+j conjugate(c1),
20:     (u,v) ~ (u e^{i phi},e^{-i phi}v),
21:     q=uv,       h=u i u^{-1},       k=v^{-1} i v.
22: 
23: The first resolution has base h and real normal coordinate q; the
24: second has base k and the same real normal coordinate q. These are actual
25: products: choose a unit representative u of h and set v=u^{-1}q in the
26: first case; choose a unit representative v of k and set u=qv^{-1} in the
27: second. A different representative changes only the displayed auxiliary
28: S^1 orbit. Both inverse constructions are smooth in the quotient charts.
29: 
30: The exact boundary map is therefore
31: 
32:     tau(h,q)=(q^{-1} h q,q).                              (FG.1)
33: 
34: It extends over D^3 times S^3 by
35: 
36:     T(y,q)=(q^{-1} y q,q),   y in Im(H), |y|<=1.          (FG.2)
37: 
38: The map is smooth at y=0, and its inverse is
39: (y,q) -> (q y q^{-1},q). It preserves the product orientation.
40: Complex orientations must be compared in these same products; changing
41: the quaternion convention in only one normal product would change the
42: displayed signs.
43: 
44: ## 2. Explicit relative generators and an exact conjugation
45: 
46: Choose an SO(3)-equivariant smooth degree-one map
47: 
48:     zeta:(D^3,S^2)->(S^3,1)
49: 
50: constant on a boundary collar. One explicit choice is
51: zeta(y)=cos(phi(r))-sin(phi(r)) y/r, with phi decreasing from pi to
52: zero, linear to first order at the centre and flat near the boundary;
53: the odd Taylor conditions at the centre make the formula smooth.
54: It commutes with y and satisfies
55: zeta(q y q^{-1})=q zeta(y)q^{-1}. Define actual relative maps
56: 
57:     L(y,q)=(y,zeta(y)q),     R(y,q)=(y,q zeta(y)).         (FG.3)
58: 
59: They commute and extend over D^3 times D^4 by the same quaternionic
60: multiplications. Thus their extensions to the boundary S^6 of that
61: seven-disc are isotopically trivial in the standard sphere filling.
62: Their integer powers and products have the same explicit extensions.
63: 
64: Substitution, with no isotopy, gives
65: 
66:     T L T^{-1}=R.                                        (FG.4)
67: 
68: The calculation uses both equivariance of zeta and the commutation of
69: zeta(y) with y. The localized maps double to the usual quaternion maps
70: on S^3 times S^3, up to isotopy through degree-one choices of zeta.
71: 
72: ## 3. The central relation and its orientation limitation
73: 
74: The primary paper of Krylov, arXiv:math/0107005, defines
75: A(s,t)=(tst^{-1},t), B(s,t)=(s,sts^{-1}), Y(s,t)=(s,st).
76: Its Theorems 2--3 give the central commutator C=[A,B], of order 28,
77: and the relation AY=YAB. The proof establishes that C generates Theta_7;
78: its last orientation choice on printed p12 is explicitly adjustable.
79: Consequently this note defines C by that commutator and does not silently
80: equate it with the oriented generator used elsewhere in this run.
81: 
82: Our T=A^{-1}, L=Y and R=YB^{-1}. Hence, in the doubled mapping class
83: group, direct use of those relations gives
84: 
85:     T R T^{-1}=L^{-1}R^2 C,
86:     T(L^m R^n C^s)T^{-1}=L^{-n}R^{m+2n}C^{s+n}.          (FG.5)
87: 
88: The first relative map in (FG.5) is fixed on the boundary because it is
89: a conjugate of a relative map. The extension-by-identity injection of
90: relative pseudo-isotopy classes into the double is Krylov,
91: arXiv:math/0409342, Proposition1. Its Lemma3 identifies the relative
92: group in this dimension as Theta_7 plus two infinite cyclic factors.
93: Here those factors are represented by (FG.3): their evaluation on the
94: relative D^3 cycle gives the sum m+n, and their normal derivative
95: difference generates the kernel of that evaluation. The central
96: disc-supported classes are unchanged by conjugation with T.
97: 
98: ## 4. The stable derivative detects the second coefficient
99: 
100: Use the Cartesian frame on D^3 and the left-invariant quaternion frame
101: on S^3. Along L, the derivative matrix has diagonal blocks I_3,I_3
102: and a lower off-diagonal block. Scaling that lower block to zero is a
103: homotopy through invertible matrices, fixed at the boundary. Thus its
104: relative stable derivative is zero.
105: 
106: Along R, the same matrix has diagonal blocks I_3,Ad(zeta^{-1}); its
107: off-diagonal block is removed by the same explicit homotopy. The
108: stabilization of Ad:S^3->SO(3) represents twice a generator of pi_3(SO).
109: This factor follows from the adjoint rank-three bundle: for an SU(2)
110: bundle with Chern roots x,-x, the complexified adjoint has roots
111: 2x,0,-2x, so p1(ad)=-4c2 whereas the quaternionic real four-plane has
112: p1=-2c2. Choose the stable generator by the latter convention.
113: 
114: The relative stable derivative of L^m R^n is therefore -2n. This
115: calculation is in the fixed product framing. A change of the boundary
116: framing can change its relation with the variation integer; it cannot
117: be discarded before identifying the actual Cox products.
118: 
119: ## 5. The actual remaining comparison
120: 
121: The exact flop map (FG.1) replaces the previously uncomputed local
122: boundary identification by an explicit conjugation. For the actual
123: family, the remaining tasks are to extend that product marking to its
124: exterior and evaluate the relative stable derivative there, with all
125: finite caps and the period reindexing retained. The ordinary torus
126: tangent matrices are triangular and the nodal maps in
127: notes/part3_nodal_frames.md remove their parameter dependence on the
128: punctured overlaps. The still required step is their global gluing to
129: the boundary framing in (FG.1). Absolute vanishing of p1 does not supply
130: that relative comparison.
131: 
132: The other orientation check is the sign of C relative to the run's
133: positive Eells--Kuiper generator. No sign is needed to recognize the
134: zero coefficient, but a nonzero coefficient requires this check. The
135: manuscript does not assign a general-fan value before these tasks are
136: completed.
